# Skill Lifecycle — verify & learn in the loop

Project-skills (`.metaproject/project-skills/<module>/<entity>/`) capture how a
module/component/store/service actually works. They drift as code changes.
Keeping them true is an **agent-loop** responsibility: the implementer, reviewer,
or orchestrator who is already in the code catches drift at the right moment,
with context. It is NOT a git hook — the `gdskills` post-commit hook only runs
`skills verify --all --dry-run` as an advisory staleness signal and never
mutates.

## Verify — cheap, inline, before you trust a skill

Whoever is about to rely on a project-skill verifies it first:

- Find it: `keryx skills route <target>`.
- Check freshness: `keryx skills verify <module>/<skill>` (or
  `skill-verify-skill`). It classifies the skill `fresh | stale | needs-review |
  blocked` from its required files, metadata, manifest registration, target
  path, and evidence artifacts (graph, ctx, wiki, health, memory). It does not
  read the skill's claims or compare them with the code.
- If not `fresh`: do NOT follow it blindly — verify each claim against the code
  and record the drift so it feeds the learn step below.

Verification is read-only and inline; it does not need a subagent.

## Learn — triggered by drift, produces a proposal, applied with review

Trigger `learn` when drift is **confirmed**, not on every commit:

- a reviewer finds a recurring issue a project-skill should have prevented;
- an implementer's work diverged from what a skill documents;
- `verify` reported `stale` / `needs-review` for a skill in scope.

Flow:

1. `keryx skills learn --from-review <report> --skill <m>/<s>` (or
   `--from-health` / `--from-test` / `--from-failure` / `--from-memory`) — writes
   a **proposal** (JSON). Nothing is mutated yet.
2. Read the proposal, then `keryx skills learn apply <proposal.json>`
   (version bump + `skill-changelog.md` with provenance, respects manual
   sections, file-locked).

`learn` is bounded, mechanical synthesis. **Dispatch it as a subagent at
`model_tier: light`, resolved via `keryx review tier --findings 1 --diff-lines 0`
per `model-selection.mdc` — do not pick a model by hand, and do not substitute
a different `review tier` invocation (e.g. `--scope narrow`, which resolves
heavier than `light`).** The flagship's only job here is to review the
proposal before apply.

### Passive observation is not mutation (W3, decision D-3)

A hook registered under the shell hook runtime (W6) may append an
**observation event** to `.metaproject/data/learning/observations/` — the
shipped `keryx.learning-observer` hook does exactly this, on
`tool-start`/`tool-complete`/`tool-failed`/`user-prompt`/`session-start`/
`turn-stop`/`session-end`. This is explicitly permitted from a hook, unlike
every other write this rule governs, because it changes no skill, rule,
agent, or memory entry — it only accumulates evidence (redacted, digest-only)
that a human will later decide about.

`keryx learn extract`, which turns accumulated observations into a
`status: candidate` `learned-pattern` record under
`.metaproject/data/learning/candidates/`, is a separate, **command- or
schedule-triggered** step — never run inside a hook. It still produces no
`accepted` state and mutates no skill/rule/agent/memory file by itself.

Everything from `keryx learn accept` onward — Accept, project-skill Apply,
reviewer-profile Apply, Promote, Graduate apply — remains agent-loop,
human-triggered work under the rule above, with the same "dispatch as a
light-tier subagent, review before apply" discipline already specified for
`skills learn apply`. Each of accept/promote/graduate-apply additionally
refuses outright outside a real interactive terminal (no bypass flag).

## Never

- Do not put `learn` (or any mutation) in a hook — **passive observation is
  the single exception** (see above): a hook may append an observation event,
  nothing more. `keryx learn extract` also never runs inside a hook, even
  though it produces no `accepted` state.
- Do not `apply` a proposal without reading it.
- Do not trust a `stale` / `needs-review` skill as ground truth.
